{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Cart page
  Phase 8.

  WHY A CART PAGE IS IN THIS PHASE AT ALL.
  The Phase 8 scope is the product page, the shopping interaction, the cart
  drawer and add-to-cart. A cart PAGE is not named — but it is the destination
  of the very form this phase builds. With JavaScript off, the product form
  posts natively to /cart/add and Shopify redirects to /cart, and Shopify
  serves an error page for any storefront route whose template is missing
  (Phase 1 SHOP-09). Without templates/cart.json the no-JavaScript path this
  phase is required to keep working would end on an error page. The drawer's
  own View cart link has the same problem.

  So this is deliberately the lean version: the same cart lines, the same
  totals, the same controls, no new components, and no design that a later
  cart phase would have to undo. The remaining templates SHOP-09 lists —
  collection, list-collections, search, page, 404, password, gift card and the
  customer set — are still missing and still belong to their own phases.

  IT IS A RENDER TARGET FOR ITS OWN AJAX.
  [data-cart-page-inner] is the node assets/cart.js replaces after a quantity
  change or a removal made on this page. Without it the script would intercept
  both controls and then have nowhere to put the answer — the page would show a
  cart that no longer existed, and, worse, its positional updates[] inputs
  would no longer line up with the server's lines, so Checkout would apply each
  surviving quantity to the wrong product. The heading and the error line sit
  outside that node, for the same reason the drawer's do.

  The cart drawer is NOT rendered on this template. Two views of one cart on
  one page can disagree, and they gave every line a duplicate DOM id.
{%- endcomment -%}

{{ 'section-main-cart.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

<div
  class="main-cart surface-dark"
  data-cart-page
  data-cart-page-section="{{ section.id }}"
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <div class="main-cart__inner container">
    <h1 class="main-cart__title" tabindex="-1" id="CartPageTitle">{{ 'cart.title' | t }}</h1>

    {%- comment -%}
      The visible failure line, outside the swapped node so a message survives
      the re-render that follows the request which produced it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <p class="main-cart__error" data-cart-error role="alert" hidden></p>

    <div data-cart-page-inner>
      {%- if cart.item_count > 0 -%}
        <form
          action="{{ routes.cart_url }}"
          method="post"
          id="CartPageForm"
          class="main-cart__form"
          data-cart-form
        >
          <ul class="main-cart__lines" role="list">
            {%- for item in cart.items -%}
              {% render 'cart-line-item',
                item: item,
                index: forloop.index,
                form_id: 'CartPageForm',
                scope: 'CartPage',
                eager: forloop.first
              %}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </ul>

          <div class="main-cart__summary">
            {%- comment -%}
              Phase 14. The same note the drawer renders, from the same
              snippet, so a correction to one reaches the other.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if section.settings.show_note -%}
              {% render 'cart-note', id: 'CartPageNote' %}
            {%- endif -%}

            {% render 'cart-totals' %}

            {%- comment -%}
              First submit in the form, so Enter in a quantity field applies the
              change rather than jumping to checkout. It is removed once the
              cart script is running, because there it does nothing: changes are
              applied as they are made and this page re-renders itself.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button type="submit" name="update" class="button button--secondary main-cart__update">
              {{ 'cart.update' | t }}
            </button>

            <button type="submit" name="checkout" class="button button--primary button--full">
              {{ 'cart.checkout' | t }}
            </button>

            {%- comment -%}
              Shopify's additional accelerated checkout buttons. Unlike the
              product form's payment_button, the cart HAS a documented guard
              object, so this renders only when the store actually has them.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- if additional_checkout_buttons -%}
              <div class="main-cart__accelerated">
                {{ content_for_additional_checkout_buttons }}
              </div>
            {%- endif -%}

            {%- comment -%}
              Phase 14. Continue shopping, which this page had no route to at
              all: its only controls were Update and Checkout, so a customer
              who did not want to check out yet had nothing but the browser's
              Back button — which the brief rules out by name.

              A LINK, unlike the drawer's. The drawer is a layer over the page
              the customer was shopping and closing it returns them there;
              arriving on /cart is a navigation, and there is nothing to return
              to. It goes wherever the merchant sends an empty cart, because
              that is the same question asked twice, and it falls back to the
              home page for the reason snippets/cart-empty-state.liquid
              records.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- assign shop_on_url = section.settings.empty_link | default: routes.root_url -%}
            <a class="main-cart__continue" href="{{ shop_on_url }}">
              {{ 'cart.continue_shopping' | t }}
            </a>
          </div>
        </form>
      {%- else -%}
        {% render 'cart-empty-state',
          url: section.settings.empty_link,
          label: section.settings.empty_link_label
        %}
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Cart",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "main-cart-section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "empty_link",
      "label": "Where the shopping buttons go",
      "info": "Used by both the empty-cart button and the Continue shopping link. Leave empty to use the home page. The cart drawer has its own copy of this setting."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "empty_link_label",
      "label": "Empty-cart button label",
      "default": "Shop the collection",
      "info": "The cart drawer has its own copy of this setting."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_note",
      "label": "Let customers add an order note",
      "default": false,
      "info": "Adds Shopify's own cart note. Turn this on only if you act on what customers write there — a field nobody reads is worse than no field. The cart drawer has its own copy of this setting."
    }
  ],
  "enabled_on": {
    "templates": ["cart"]
  }
}
{% endschema %}
